  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">PASSIM 4 &#x2013; First Health Assistance for Rescue at Sea Operations 4</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In the context of rescue at sea operations and landings on the Southern Italian shores, the Italian Coast Guard personnel is providing humanitarian response to newly arrived vulnerable migrants in cooperation with doctors and nurses from the CISOM (Italian Relief Corps of the Order to Malta). In such circumstances, linguistic and cultural barriers can significantly impact the appropriateness and effectiveness of rescue at sea protocols and identification of health needs, which can then influence access to care and health services. Moreover, having multiple actors involved in the phases of rescue at sea, landings, and the following transfers to hotspots can generate further disorientation for beneficiaries. The PASSIM 4 project aims to continue providing, as in the previous project phases, implemented starting from 2016, cultural mediation services supporting national authorities responsible for rescue at the Mediterranean Sea and continuity of care at the arrival and landing on the coasts of Sicily and Calabria. 
 
In pursuit of this objective, IOM will provide cultural mediation during rescue-at-sea operations. The presence of 22 cultural mediators will have a two-fold positive effect. Firstly, it will bridge possible different cultural approaches to rescue and health care provision between migrants, rescue personnel and health care providers. Secondly, it will facilitate the continuity of care from the first contact with the vulnerable migrant through landing. 
 
IOM cultural mediators will be located on the Isle of Lampedusa , Catania (Sicily), Roccella Ionica and Crotone (Calabria). They will participate in rescue operations at sea to support the health personnel of CISOM (Corpo Italiano di Soccorso dell&apos;Ordine di Malta/ Italian Relief Corps of the Order of Malta ) on board of the Italian Coast Guard patrol vessels, during disembarkation activities. Their presence aims to strengthen the cultural sensitivity and appropriateness of care and vulnerability assessments.</narrative>
  </description>
